{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1275 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "e8edb1a2dd425e1581b4367906be3d00d1862b98166e5b5a448518e591d34cfc" :edb/db-before-t 1274 :edb/db-after-t 1275 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534587 50 #inst "2026-10-08T01:44:56.069Z" 13194139534587 true] [17592186128935 1100 "What are the cylindrical polar coordinates of the point $N$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1981/Source/Images/deac7441155c1440a00a786c590e7f03.png)" 13194139534587 false] [17592186128935 1100 "What are the cylindrical polar coordinates of the point $N?$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/assets/53fada6c8e77c7c6fa2a6579b52af21ea03839e134fc2cb3d9529f6cfc01bd4b.png)" 13194139534587 true] [17592186128935 1145 "We write the position of a point in cylindrical coordinates as $(r,\\theta,z)$, where\n\n$$\nr \\geq 0\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n0 \\leq \\theta < 2\\pi\n$$\n\nAccording to the diagram, we have the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1981/Source/Images/5ea6b167ebe524618c905f0a1fe8f041.png)\n\n- The ray from the origin through $N'$ makes an angle of $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} - \\dfrac{\\pi}{3} = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n- The distance from the origin to $N'$ is $r=\\sqrt{2}$.\n- The $z$-coordinate is $z=3$ because $N$ lies $3$ units *above* the $xy$-plane.\n\nSo, the cylindrical coordinates of $N$ are\n\n$$\n\\left(\\sqrt{2}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}, 3\\right)\n$$" 13194139534587 false] [17592186128935 1145 "We write the position of a point in cylindrical coordinates as $(r,\\theta,z),$ where $r \\geq 0$ and $0 \\leq \\theta <         2\\pi.$\n\nAccording to the diagram, we have the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/assets/d60214a4b32c4462d422372aa90e78944649811ce0b1e277f07fe90663e5be94.png)\n\n  \n\n- The ray from the origin through $N'$ makes an angle of $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} - \\dfrac{\\pi}{3} = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n- The distance from the origin to $N'$ is $r=\\sqrt{2}.$\n\n- The $z$-coordinate is $z=3$ because $N$ lies $3$ units *above* the $xy$-plane.\n\nSo, the cylindrical coordinates of $N$ are $\\left(\\sqrt{2}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}, 3\\right).$" 13194139534587 true] [17592186128936 1100 "The point $A$ has Cartesian coordinates $(\\sqrt3,-3,\\sqrt2)$. What are the cylindrical polar coordinates of $A$?" 13194139534587 false] [17592186128936 1100 "The point $A$ has Cartesian coordinates $(\\sqrt3,-3,\\sqrt2).$ What are the cylindrical polar coordinates of $A?$" 13194139534587 true] [17592186128936 1145 "We write the position of a point in cylindrical coordinates as $(r,\\theta,z)$, where\n\n$$\nr \\geq 0\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n0 \\leq \\theta < 2\\pi\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are given as\n\n$$\n(x,y,z) = (\\sqrt3,-3,\\sqrt2)\n$$\n\n- From these Cartesian coordinates, we know that $z=\\sqrt{2}$.\n- To find the values of $r$ and $\\theta$, we need to find the polar coordinates of the point $(x,y) = (\\sqrt 3, -3)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nFirst, we find $r$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr &= \\sqrt{x^{2} + y^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(\\sqrt{3})^{2} + (-3)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{3 + 9} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{12} \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $(\\sqrt3,-3)$ lies in the fourth quadrant, we first need to find the reference angle $\\theta_R$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nθ_{R} &= \\arctan \\mid \\frac{y}{x} \\mid \\\\\n&= \\arctan \\mid \\frac{-3}{\\sqrt{3}} \\mid \\\\\n&= \\arctan (\\sqrt{3}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we subtract the reference angle from $2\\pi$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nθ &= 2π - θ_{R} \\\\\n&= 2π - \\frac{π}{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{5π}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the cylindrical coordinates of $A$ are\n\n$$\n\\left(2\\sqrt{3}, \\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}, \\sqrt2 \\right)\n$$" 13194139534587 false] [17592186128936 1145 "We write the position of a point in cylindrical coordinates as $(r,\\theta,z),$ where $r \\geq 0$ and $0 \\leq \\theta <    2\\pi.$\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are given as $(x,y,z) = (\\sqrt3,-3,\\sqrt2).$\n\n- From these Cartesian coordinates, we know that $z=\\sqrt{2}.$\n\n- To find the values of $r$ and $\\theta,$ we need to find the polar coordinates of the point $(x,y) = (\\sqrt 3, -3)$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nFirst, we find $r,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\sqrt{3})}^{2}+(-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{12} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $(\\sqrt3,-3)$ lies in the fourth  quadrant, we first need to find  the reference angle $\\theta_R\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{-3}{\\sqrt{3}}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we subtract the reference angle from $2\\pi\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the cylindrical coordinates of $A$ are $\\left(2\\sqrt{3}, \\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}, \\sqrt2 \\right).$" 13194139534587 true] [17592186128937 1100 "The point $P$ has cylindrical polar coordinates $\\left(4, \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}, -8 \\right)$. Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P$." 13194139534587 false] [17592186128937 1100 "The point $P$ has cylindrical polar coordinates $\\left(4, \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}, -8 \\right).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P.$" 13194139534587 true] [17592186128937 1145 "If $(r, \\theta, z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx = r\\cos\\theta, \\qquad\ny = r\\sin\\theta, \\qquad \nz = z.\n$$\n\nCalculating $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $y$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(2\\sqrt3, 2, -8).$" 13194139534587 true] [17592186128937 1145 "If $(r, \\theta, z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx = r\\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = r\\sin\\theta, \\qquad z = z\n$$\n\nCalculating $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= r\\cos θ \\\\\n&= 4\\cos (\\frac{π}{6}) \\\\\n&= 4 \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $y$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= r\\sin θ \\\\\n&= 4\\sin (\\frac{π}{6}) \\\\\n&= 4 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(2\\sqrt3, 2, -8)$." 13194139534587 false] [17592186129116 1020 #edb/ref 17592186297206 13194139534587 true] [17592186129116 1020 #edb/ref 17592186297213 13194139534587 true] [17592186129116 1020 #edb/ref 17592186297220 13194139534587 true] [17592186129116 1020 #edb/ref 17592186297227 13194139534587 true] [17592186129117 1020 #edb/ref 17592186297199 13194139534587 true] [17592186129117 1020 #edb/ref 17592186297248 13194139534587 true] [17592186129117 1020 #edb/ref 17592186297255 13194139534587 true] [17592186129117 1020 #edb/ref 17592186297262 13194139534587 true] [17592186129118 1020 #edb/ref 17592186297192 13194139534587 true] [17592186129118 1020 #edb/ref 17592186297234 13194139534587 true] [17592186129118 1020 #edb/ref 17592186297241 13194139534587 true] [17592186129796 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534587 true] [17592186129796 1100 "The point $A$ has Cartesian coordinates $(2,-2, 5)$. What are the cylindrical polar coordinates of $A$?" 13194139534587 false] [17592186129796 1100 "The point $A$ has Cartesian coordinates $(2,-2,5).$ What are the cylindrical polar coordinates of $A?$" 13194139534587 true] [17592186129796 1145 "We write the position of a point in cylindrical coordinates as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where $r\\ge 0$ and $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .$\n\nConsider the point $(x,y)=(2,-2)$ that represents the projection of $(x,y,z)=(2,-2,5)$ onto the $xy$-plane. We need to find the plane polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ of $(2,-2).$ Then, the cylindrical coordinates of the point $A$ will be $(r,\\theta ,z).$\n\nFirst, we find $r$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{(-2)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $(2,-2)$ lies in the fourth  quadrant, we first need to find  the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{-2}{2}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we subtract the reference angle from $2\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the cylindrical coordinates of $A$ are $(2\\sqrt{2},\\frac{7\\pi }{4},5).$" 13194139534587 true] [17592186129798 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534587 true] [17592186129798 1100 "The point $P$ has cylindrical polar coordinates $(4, \\frac{π}{6},-2)$. Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P$." 13194139534587 false] [17592186129798 1100 "The point $P$ has cylindrical polar coordinates $(4,\\frac{\\pi }{6},-2).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P.$" 13194139534587 true] [17592186129798 1145 "If $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z\n$$\n\nCalculating $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $y$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(2\\sqrt{3},2,-2).$" 13194139534587 true] [17592186129799 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534587 true] [17592186129799 1100 "The point $Q$ has cylindrical coordinates $(5, π, 7)$. Find the Cartesian coordinates of $Q$." 13194139534587 false] [17592186129799 1100 "The point $Q$ has cylindrical coordinates $(5,\\pi ,7).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $Q.$" 13194139534587 true] [17592186129799 1145 "If $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z\n$$\n\nCalculating $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =5\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $y$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =5\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $Q$ are $(-5,0,7).$" 13194139534587 true] [17592186129805 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534587 true] [17592186129805 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/assets/dcf326a7962c01814c937e07083193a107597e1995fb66dffd5776a40cf07a9f.png)\n\nWhat are the cylindrical polar coordinates of the point $F?$" 13194139534587 true] [17592186129805 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1981/Source/Images/q-74430.png)\n\nWhat are the cylindrical polar coordinates of the point $F$?" 13194139534587 false] [17592186129805 1145 "We write the position of a point in cylindrical coordinates as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where $r\\ge 0$ and $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .$\n\nAccording to the diagram, we have the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033855/assets/189c1f77e7d2dfd97f51e05f4be55423d100921d2dcad5cafe4be4d90463e8d8.png)\n\n \n\n- The ray from the origin through ${F}^{′}$ makes an angle of $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n- The distance from the origin to ${F}^{′}$ is $r=1.$\n\n- The $z$-coordinate is $z=-1$ because $F$ lies $1$ unit *below* the $xy$-plane.\n\nSo, the cylindrical coordinates of $F$ are $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{5},-1).$" 13194139534587 true] [17592186297192 1017 #edb/ref 17592186297193 13194139534587 true] [17592186297192 1089 "q-168896" 13194139534587 true] [17592186297192 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534587 true] [17592186297192 1100 "The point $P$ has cylindrical polar coordinates $(8,\\frac{\\pi }{6},-5).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P.$" 13194139534587 true] [17592186297192 1113 #uuid "66e7c24c-c992-5cdb-b822-0da7485aaa3f" 13194139534587 true] [17592186297192 1145 "If $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z\n$$\n\nCalculating $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating $y$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(4\\sqrt{3},4,-5).$" 13194139534587 true] [17592186297193 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297194 13194139534587 true] [17592186297193 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297195 13194139534587 true] [17592186297193 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297196 13194139534587 true] [17592186297193 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297197 13194139534587 true] [17592186297193 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297198 13194139534587 true] [17592186297193 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534587 true] [17592186297193 1041 "selection" 13194139534587 true] [17592186297193 1104 #edb/ref 17592186297197 13194139534587 true] [17592186297193 1110 #uuid "6a92fb34-2c6a-519b-a97b-65366e953f82" 13194139534587 true] [17592186297194 1034 "(2\\sqrt{3},4,-5)" 13194139534587 true] [17592186297194 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534587 true] [17592186297194 1111 #uuid "8e69a953-dd80-5431-b0f1-eb4e0406ca3a" 13194139534587 true] [17592186297195 1034 "(-4\\sqrt{3},-5,-5)" 13194139534587 true] [17592186297195 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534587 true] [17592186297195 1111 #uuid "c0602588-4c8f-544b-870e-fc2ee271fbe7" 13194139534587 true] [17592186297196 1034 "(\\sqrt{3},2,-5)" 13194139534587 true] [17592186297196 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534587 true] [17592186297196 1111 #uuid "e1614bdf-52f2-5bc5-9fa5-675143952b9e" 13194139534587 true] [17592186297197 1034 "(4\\sqrt{3},4,-5)" 13194139534587 true] [17592186297197 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534587 true] [17592186297197 1111 #uuid "5434c4e9-49c1-57ca-9078-70975dbd6444" 13194139534587 true] [17592186297198 1034 "(\\sqrt{3},-2,-5)" 13194139534587 true] [17592186297198 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534587 true] [17592186297198 1111 #uuid "316ad55e-7cc3-5104-b6f0-c46665aee988" 13194139534587 true] [17592186297199 1017 #edb/ref 17592186297200 13194139534587 true] [17592186297199 1089 "q-168898" 13194139534587 true] [17592186297199 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534587 true] [17592186297199 1100 "The point $A$ has Cartesian coordinates $(-2,2,2).$ What are the cylindrical polar coordinates of $A?$" 13194139534587 true] [17592186297199 1113 #uuid "0699ef4a-46bc-50f3-8bab-c13f8e76f124" 13194139534587 true] [17592186297199 1145 "We write the position of a point in cylindrical coordinates as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where $r\\ge 0$ and $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .$\n\nConsider the point $(x,y)=(-2,2)$ that represents the projection of $(x,y,z)=(-2,2,2)$ onto the $xy$-plane. We need to find the plane polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ of $(-2,2).$ Then, the cylindrical coordinates of the point $A$ will be $(r,\\theta ,z).$\n\nFirst, we find $r$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(-2)}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $(-2,2)$ lies in the second quadrant, we first need to find the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{2}{-2}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we subtract the reference angle from $\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the cylindrical coordinates of $A$ are $(2\\sqrt{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{4},2).$" 13194139534587 true] [17592186297200 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297201 13194139534587 true] [17592186297200 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297202 13194139534587 true] [17592186297200 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297203 13194139534587 true] [17592186297200 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297204 13194139534587 true] [17592186297200 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297205 13194139534587 true] [17592186297200 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534587 true] [17592186297200 1041 "selection" 13194139534587 true] [17592186297200 1104 #edb/ref 17592186297202 13194139534587 true] [17592186297200 1110 #uuid "8dbcc958-599e-576f-9487-b62