sider the point $(x,y)=(-1,-\\sqrt{3})$ that represents the projection of $(x,y,z)=(-1,-\\sqrt{3},2)$ onto the $xy$-plane. We need to find the plane polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ of $(-1,-\\sqrt{3}).$ Then, the cylindrical coordinates of the point $A$ will be $(r,\\theta ,z).$ \n\nFirst, we find $r$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{(-\\sqrt{3})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+3} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, since $(-1,-\\sqrt{3})$ lies in the third  quadrant, we first need to find  the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{-\\sqrt{3}}{-1}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we add the reference angle and $\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the cylindrical coordinates of $A$ are $(2,\\frac{4\\pi }{3},2).$" 13194139534587 true] [17592186297249 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297250 13194139534587 true] [17592186297249 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297251 13194139534587 true] [17592186297249 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297252 13194139534587 true] [17592186297249 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297253 13194139534587 true] [17592186297249 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297254 13194139534587 true] [17592186297249 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534587 true] [17592186297249 1041 "selection" 13194139534587 true] [17592186297249 1104 #edb/ref 17592186297252 13194139534587 true] [17592186297249 1110 #uuid "3bac72af-705c-53cc-a679-e95f5e2f1ca1" 13194139534587 true] [17592186297250 1034 "(1,\\frac{7\\pi }{6},2)" 13194139534587 true] [17592186297250 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534587 true] [17592186297250 1111 #uuid "8be40b28-bc94-5725-b520-bd13f6a6497e" 13194139534587 true] [17592186297251 1034 "(2,\\frac{7\\pi }{6},2)" 13194139